body: "Narrow accuracy issue, **not** a gate failure — stating that up front because the first reading looks alarming and is wrong.\n\n## What happens\n\n`pm ops merge-receipts` reports `fence.status: \"drift\"` on pm-rl, pm-gantt-chart and pm-vcs, with:\n\n```\nstale_patterns:\n  - \".agents/pm/extensions/**\" -merge\n  - \".agents/pm/extensions/.managed-extensions.json\" merge=pm-json\n```\n\nThose two patterns are **not stale**. Verified against the CLI directly — a fresh `pm init` + `pm merge install` in an empty git repo writes both:\n\n| pm version | writes the two `.agents/pm/extensions` lines? |\n| --- | --- |\n| 2026.8.11 (latest published) | **yes** |\n| 2026.8.7 (fleet pin) | **yes** |\n| 2026.8.6 | no |\n\nSo the expected-pattern list here is behind the CLI: it treats patterns the current `pm merge install` actively writes as leftovers from an older layout.\n\n## Why the impact is limited, and why it is still worth fixing\n\nThe gate logic is already right, and `index.ts:1768-1775` says so explicitly — `missing_patterns` means a folder falls back to git line-merging and **fails** the gate, while purely `stale_patterns` drift is over-coverage and is reported without failing. The run confirms it: `unprotected_fence: 0`, exit 0.\n\nThe problem is what the label invites. `stale` reads as \"safe to remove\", and these two lines are what keep `.agents/pm/extensions/**` out of git's line-based merge. Someone tidying a reported drift would delete exactly the protection the audit exists to preserve — and the audit would then go quiet, because absence of those patterns is not reported as `missing` either (they are not in the expected set at all).\n\nIt also means every correctly-configured repo in the fleet reports `drift`, so `drifted_fence` is noise rather than signal: 3 of 3 repos scanned, all correct.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nAdd both patterns to the expected fence set, which makes `drifted_fence` go to 0 for correctly-configured repos and lets a real drift stand out. If the set is meant to track a specific pm version rather than the current one, it would help to say which version it encodes, so a future divergence reads as a version gap rather than as repo drift.\n\n## Repro\n\n```bash\ncd \"$(mktemp -d)\" && git init -q .\npm init fnc && pm merge install\ngrep -c \"agents/pm/extensions\" .gitattributes   # => 2 on 2026.8.7 and 2026.8.11\n```\n\n---\n\n### GitHub comments (2)\n\n> **@coderabbitai[bot]** (2026-08-11T19:50:34Z)\n>\n> <!-- This is an auto-generated issue plan by CodeRabbit -->\n> <details>\n> <summary>🔗 Related PRs</summary>\n> \n> unbraind/pm-ops#28 - chore(deps): adopt pm-cli 2026.7.28 [merged]\n> unbraind/pm-ops#38 - Gate durable PM health and audit workspace attribution [merged]\n> unbraind/pm-ops#39 - Enforce exact all-source coverage and pm CLI 2026.8.7 compatibility [merged]\n> unbraind/pm-ops#41 - Make the throughput fixtures relative so the daily release stops failing on the calendar [merged]\n> unbraind/pm-ops#42 - Reconcile the manifest with the 2026.8.10 already published to npm [merged]\n> </details>\n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary>📝 Issue Planner</summary>\n> \n> <sub>Check the box below or use the `@coderabbitai plan` command to generate an implementation plan and prompts that you can use with your favorite coding assistant.</sub>\n> \n> - [ ] <!-- {\"checkboxId\": \"8d4f2b9c-3e1a-4f7c-a9b2-d5e8f1c4a7b9\"} --> Create Plan\n> </details>\n> \n> \n> ---\n> <details>\n> <summary> 🧪 Issue enrichment is currently in open beta.</summary>\n> \n> \n> You can configure auto-planning by selecting labels in the issue_enrichment configuration.\n> \n> To disable automatic issue enrichment, add the following to your `.coderabbit.yaml`:\n> ```yaml\n> issue_enrichment:\n>   auto_enrich:\n>     enabled: false\n> ```\n> </details>\n> \n> 💬 Have feedback or questions? Drop into our [discord](https://discord.gg/coderabbit)!\n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-08-17T10:42:32Z)\n>\n> The issue describes real drift reported by `pm ops merge-receipts` on the runtime observed when it was filed: two canonical `.agents/pm/extensions` fence patterns flagged as `stale_patterns`, with the over-coverage gap noted in the body. Re-checking against the current tree, that behavior no longer reproduces.\n> \n> **Current tree:** pm-ops `2026.8.17`, origin/main commit `2332a64a0471fe5414ffa087667d9720066426c0`; peer floor `@unbrained/pm-cli >=2026.8.15`, dev dependency `2026.8.16`.\n> \n> **Functional verification** (real SDK host harness, synthetic git/pm repo):\n> \n> 1. Fresh `pm init` + `pm merge install`, committed canonical fence, then `ops merge-receipts` → `fence.status: \"ok\"`, `missing_patterns: []`, `stale_patterns: []`, `drifted_fence: 0`, `unprotected_fence: 0`.\n> 2. Removed exactly the two `.agents/pm/extensions/` fence lines and reran → `fence.status: \"drift\"`, `missing_patterns` exactly `\".agents/pm/extensions/**\" -merge` and `\".agents/pm/extensions/.managed-extensions.json\" merge=pm-json`, `stale_patterns: []`, `drifted_fence: 1`, `unprotected_fence: 1`; the non-warn-only command was rejected by the uncovered-fence gate.\n> \n> So the two patterns the issue asked to add to the expected fence set are now produced by the current SDK contract, and their absence is reported as `missing_patterns` (which fails the gate) rather than ignored as `stale_patterns` — closing the exact over-coverage gap described here.\n> \n> **Source:** pm-ops delegates fence construction entirely to the SDK `auditMergeAttributeFence`; the current SDK `buildMergeAttributePatterns` emits both `.agents/pm/extensions/**` (`-merge`) and `.agents/pm/extensions/.managed-extensions.json` (`merge=pm-json`). There is no pm-ops-owned expected-pattern list to update — the expected set is supplied by the SDK.\n> \n> No pm-ops PR is needed. Closing as resolved against the current supported SDK contract.\n>"
